                    coach_r2_committed:     `<strong>You committed cleanly and built the right shape.</strong> Taking GeoNorth deep and declining AtlasMap forecloses one tie but earns trust and a defensible position — far better than straddling and being distrusted by both.`,
                    coach_r2_straddle:      `<strong>You quietly allied with both rival mapping firms.</strong> AtlasMap will find out — and when it does you'll face an exclusivity ultimatum and a threat to pull the data feed three of your OEM programs depend on. The conflicting tie can't be straddled cost-free. Which firm should you have committed to?`,
                    coach_r2_neither:       `<strong>Sidestepping the conflict is safe but thin.</strong> Taking neither mapping firm avoids the fight, but you also forgo two of the most valuable broker seats on the map. Sometimes the right move is to commit to one and manage the foreclosure.`,
                    coach_r2_periphery:     `<strong>You loaded the spend onto the cheap periphery.</strong> Light co-marketing ties across many partners look busy but don't buy a defensible position. Weight the budget toward the structural-hole seats where the rent actually accrues.`,
                    coach_r3_reframe:       `<strong>You held your committed choice and re-priced it.</strong> Re-confirming the relationship rather than caving keeps the portfolio intact and signals you negotiate from position, not panic — that's how you manage coopetition without collapsing the portfolio.`,
                    coach_r3_cave:          `<strong>You caved to whoever shouted loudest.</strong> Granting blanket exclusivity under pressure hands the value to the partner and strips your broker position of optionality. Re-confirm your committed choice and re-price — don't surrender the position.`,
                    coach_r3_ignore:        `<strong>Still straddling, now under an ultimatum.</strong> Denying everything to both sides reads as duplicity — both partners distrust you and one will retaliate. You tried to avoid a choice and now it costs you twice.`,
                    coach_r3_lockin:        `<strong>That's how durable brokerage works.</strong> Embedding Anatolia technically and contractually makes you costly to bypass — so when the two partners meet directly, the cheapest path still runs through you. Lock-in, not broadcasting, is what gives broker rent a long shelf life.`,
                    coach_r3_broadcast:     `<strong>You announced the connection in a press release — and they've now met without you twice.</strong> Visible brokerage advertises exactly how bypassable you are; your broker rent has a shelf life of about two quarters. Make yourself hard to bypass instead of broadcasting that you aren't.`,
                    coach_r3_withdraw:      `<strong>Withdrawing protects nothing.</strong> Stop introducing partners and you stop being the broker at all — you've conceded the structural-hole position rather than defending it. The answer is lock-in, not retreat.`,
                    coach_r3_leak:          `<strong>You shared your full sensor-fusion roadmap to win the tie.</strong> That partner is also a sub-supplier to your biggest rival — in eighteen months your roadmap is feeding the competitor. Innovation access is real, but unbounded disclosure is a leak. Get the access with containment.`,
                    coach_r3_unfunded:      `<strong>You over-committed the budget and the lock-in is underfunded.</strong> You spent the $28M building the portfolio and now can't fully fund the contracts and embedded standards that make the rent durable. Reserve capacity for appropriation, not just acquisition.`,
                    coach_r3_nolockin:      `<strong>Almost no investment in lock-in.</strong> Without embedded standards and switching costs, your position is easy to bypass and the rent erodes within a few quarters. Lock-in is the engine that converts a good position into durable rent.`,
                    coach_r4_mechanisms:    `<strong>Now the rent is actually yours.</strong> Exclusivity clauses, embedded standards and switching costs convert the joint value the network creates into value Anatolia captures. You built the value AND closed the till — that's the create-to-appropriate move done right.`,
                    coach_r4_goodwill:      `<strong>Your portfolio creates about $60M of joint value — for the ecosystem.</strong> Loosely governed deals with no exclusivity, no embedded standard and no switching cost leave the rent flowing to your partners. You built the value and left the till open. What mechanism makes it yours?`,
                    coach_r4_undercut:      `<strong>Undercutting on price abandons the whole thesis.</strong> Competing on volume ignores the position you spent a quarter building and pushes you straight back toward the 6–7% commodity margin. Your advantage is the network, not the price tag.`,
                    coach_r4_double:        `<strong>You doubled down where the rent is and cut the vanity ties.</strong> Concentrating on the high-rent broker seats and exiting the weak ties — backed by real appropriation mechanics — is exactly how you defend position as the network matures.`,
                    coach_r4_expand:        `<strong>Out-numbering the rival is the vanity trap again.</strong> More ties dilute your attention and your rent; the rival wins by position, not headcount. Defend by deepening centrality where it's real, not by signing everyone in sight.`,
                    coach_r4_hold:          `<strong>Holding everything unchanged ignores the rival's move.</strong> The network has shifted and some of your ties are now vanity; standing still lets the rent erode. Re-optimise — double down on the rent seats, exit the weak ones.`,
                    coach_r4_vanity:        `<strong>A partner-count target is the wrong feedback rule.</strong> Counting ties every quarter rewards exactly the connectedness-not-centrality error. Set an aspiration on design-win and centrality — search below it, consolidate above it.`,

